{"module":"courts","voice":"male","duration":194.1,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Courts: juries, sentences and the death penalty","start":0.0,"end":3.45,"words":[[0.05,0.41],[0.67,1.25],[1.51,2.16],[2.16,2.38],[2.38,2.52],[2.52,2.74],[2.74,3.25]]},{"el":"intro","text":"After a 911 call and an arrest comes the courtroom.","start":4.25,"end":8.33,"words":[[4.29,4.57],[4.57,4.71],[4.71,6.05],[6.05,6.26],[6.26,6.47],[6.47,6.61],[6.61,6.96],[6.96,7.24],[7.24,7.38],[7.38,7.88]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Each step, from the charge to the jury to the sentence, is a place where discretion can carry bias.","start":8.68,"end":14.36,"words":[[8.72,8.91],[8.91,9.23],[9.45,9.7],[9.7,9.83],[9.83,10.14],[10.14,10.27],[10.27,10.4],[10.4,10.77],[10.77,10.9],[10.9,11.03],[11.03,11.53],[11.75,11.88],[11.88,12.0],[12.0,12.32],[12.32,12.51],[12.51,13.08],[13.08,13.27],[13.27,13.52],[13.52,13.9]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Who sits on the jury","start":14.71,"end":15.99,"words":[[14.74,14.87],[14.87,15.14],[15.14,15.27],[15.27,15.4],[15.4,15.8]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The Supreme Court struck down laws barring Black jurors in 1880 (Strauder v. West Virginia), but prosecutors kept removing Black jurors with “peremptory” strikes.","start":16.69,"end":27.86,"words":[[16.74,16.89],[16.89,17.34],[17.34,17.57],[17.57,17.95],[17.95,18.25],[18.25,18.48],[18.48,18.85],[18.85,19.16],[19.16,19.76],[19.76,19.91],[19.91,20.59],[20.59,21.04],[21.04,21.27],[21.65,21.95],[21.95,22.63],[22.9,23.12],[23.12,23.95],[23.95,24.26],[24.26,24.78],[24.78,25.09],[25.09,25.69],[25.69,25.92],[25.92,26.67],[26.67,27.28]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Batson v. Kentucky (1986) banned race-based strikes, yet they continued: in Flowers v. Mississippi (2019) one prosecutor had struck 41 of 42 Black prospective jurors over six trials of the same man.","start":28.21,"end":43.89,"words":[[28.27,28.7],[28.7,28.92],[29.28,29.78],[29.78,30.86],[31.11,31.4],[31.4,32.05],[32.05,32.62],[32.88,33.09],[33.09,33.31],[33.31,34.03],[34.28,34.42],[34.42,34.85],[34.85,35.07],[35.43,36.01],[36.01,36.94],[37.19,37.41],[37.41,38.13],[38.13,38.35],[38.35,38.71],[38.71,39.21],[39.21,39.35],[39.35,39.79],[39.79,40.07],[40.07,40.79],[40.79,41.37],[41.37,41.66],[41.66,41.95],[41.95,42.45],[42.45,42.59],[42.59,42.74],[42.74,43.03],[43.03,43.31]]},{"el":"s0","text":"A 2012 study found that juries drawn from all-white pools convicted Black defendants significantly more often than white defendants; the gap disappeared when the pool included even one Black member.","start":44.24,"end":56.63,"words":[[44.27,44.42],[44.42,45.23],[45.23,45.6],[45.6,45.97],[45.97,46.19],[46.19,46.71],[46.71,47.01],[47.01,47.3],[47.3,47.74],[47.74,48.04],[48.04,48.7],[48.7,49.0],[49.0,49.74],[49.74,50.7],[50.7,50.85],[50.85,51.14],[51.14,51.36],[51.36,51.66],[51.66,52.47],[52.73,52.88],[52.88,53.1],[53.1,53.69],[53.69,53.91],[53.91,54.06],[54.06,54.28],[54.28,54.87],[54.87,55.17],[55.17,55.39],[55.39,55.68],[55.68,56.13]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Charges and sentences","start":57.53,"end":59.3,"words":[[57.57,58.12],[58.12,58.36],[58.36,59.07]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Prosecutors decide what to charge, and that choice often sets the sentence.","start":60.0,"end":65.21,"words":[[60.03,60.85],[60.85,61.3],[61.3,61.53],[61.53,61.68],[61.68,62.13],[62.4,62.62],[62.62,62.85],[62.85,63.22],[63.22,63.52],[63.52,63.83],[63.83,63.98],[63.98,64.58]]},{"el":"s1","text":"A 2014 study of federal cases found that Black men were more likely than similar white men to be charged with offenses carrying mandatory minimums, which explained much of the sentence gap.","start":65.56,"end":78.54,"words":[[65.61,65.77],[65.77,66.64],[66.64,67.04],[67.04,67.2],[67.2,67.76],[67.76,68.24],[68.24,68.64],[68.64,68.88],[68.88,69.19],[69.19,69.43],[69.43,69.59],[69.59,69.75],[69.75,70.23],[70.23,70.47],[70.47,70.95],[70.95,71.27],[71.27,71.51],[71.51,71.67],[71.67,71.82],[71.82,72.3],[72.3,72.54],[72.54,73.1],[73.1,73.66],[73.66,74.37],[74.37,75.09],[75.37,75.69],[75.69,76.41],[76.41,76.73],[76.73,76.89],[76.89,77.04],[77.04,77.6],[77.6,77.92]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The U.S. Sentencing Commission found Black men received sentences about 19% longer than similar white men (2017).","start":78.89,"end":88.07,"words":[[78.93,79.09],[79.09,79.48],[79.87,80.58],[80.58,81.13],[81.13,81.52],[81.52,81.83],[81.83,82.07],[82.07,82.54],[82.54,83.25],[83.25,83.64],[83.64,84.66],[84.66,85.05],[85.05,85.29],[85.29,85.76],[85.76,86.07],[86.07,86.31],[86.31,87.49]]},{"el":"h2","text":"The death penalty","start":88.97,"end":90.39,"words":[[89.02,89.22],[89.22,89.51],[89.51,90.19]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Supreme Court halted executions in 1972 (Furman v. Georgia), partly over arbitrary and racially skewed sentencing, then allowed them to resume in 1976.","start":91.09,"end":102.95,"words":[[91.14,91.28],[91.28,91.72],[91.72,91.94],[91.94,92.38],[92.38,93.19],[93.19,93.34],[93.34,94.51],[94.51,94.88],[94.88,95.1],[95.46,95.98],[96.23,96.6],[96.6,96.89],[96.89,97.48],[97.48,97.7],[97.7,98.21],[98.21,98.58],[98.58,99.31],[99.57,99.79],[99.79,100.16],[100.16,100.38],[100.38,100.52],[100.52,100.96],[100.96,101.11],[101.11,102.5]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In McCleskey v. Kemp (1987) it accepted a study showing that Georgia defendants who killed white victims were far more likely to be sentenced to death than those who killed Black victims, but upheld the sentence because no intent was proven in McCleskey's case.","start":103.3,"end":120.83,"words":[[103.35,103.51],[103.51,104.14],[104.14,104.38],[104.77,105.09],[105.09,106.35],[106.63,106.79],[106.79,107.42],[107.42,107.58],[107.58,107.97],[107.97,108.37],[108.37,108.6],[108.6,109.08],[109.08,109.87],[109.87,110.03],[110.03,110.34],[110.34,110.66],[110.66,111.21],[111.21,111.37],[111.37,111.53],[111.53,111.68],[111.68,112.16],[112.16,112.31],[112.31,112.47],[112.47,113.1],[113.1,113.26],[113.26,113.5],[113.5,113.74],[113.74,114.05],[114.05,114.21],[114.21,114.53],[114.53,114.84],[114.84,115.47],[115.75,115.99],[115.99,116.46],[116.46,116.62],[116.62,117.17],[117.17,117.57],[117.57,117.8],[117.8,118.28],[118.28,118.51],[118.51,118.99],[118.99,119.14],[119.14,119.85],[119.85,120.25]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Justice Powell, who wrote the decision, later said it was the one vote he would change.","start":121.18,"end":126.76,"words":[[121.22,121.72],[121.72,122.14],[122.38,122.52],[122.52,122.81],[122.81,122.95],[122.95,123.51],[123.75,124.1],[124.1,124.32],[124.32,124.46],[124.46,124.67],[124.67,124.81],[124.81,125.02],[125.02,125.3],[125.3,125.44],[125.44,125.65],[125.65,126.21]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Bail and lawyers","start":127.66,"end":129.26,"words":[[127.72,128.17],[128.17,128.51],[128.51,129.07]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Gideon v. Wainwright (1963) guaranteed a lawyer to anyone charged with a felony who can't afford one, but public defenders often carry far more cases than professional standards recommend (National Public Defense Workload Study, 2023).","start":129.96,"end":146.42,"words":[[130.04,130.56],[130.56,130.78],[131.15,131.74],[131.74,132.92],[133.18,133.77],[133.77,133.92],[133.92,134.21],[134.21,134.36],[134.36,134.8],[134.8,135.25],[135.25,135.47],[135.47,135.62],[135.62,136.06],[136.06,136.21],[136.21,136.5],[136.5,136.8],[136.8,137.09],[137.35,137.57],[137.57,138.02],[138.02,138.61],[138.61,138.9],[138.9,139.2],[139.2,139.35],[139.35,139.49],[139.49,139.94],[139.94,140.16],[140.16,140.9],[140.9,141.49],[141.49,142.08],[142.08,142.6],[142.6,143.04],[143.04,143.48],[143.48,144.0],[144.0,144.44],[144.7,145.88]]},{"el":"s3","text":"People who can't pay bail stay in jail before trial, which can cost them jobs and homes, and pressures them to plead guilty.","start":146.77,"end":155.18,"words":[[146.81,147.21],[147.21,147.36],[147.36,147.68],[147.68,147.91],[147.91,148.23],[148.23,148.54],[148.54,148.7],[148.7,149.09],[149.09,149.4],[149.4,149.95],[150.23,150.54],[150.54,150.78],[150.78,151.09],[151.09,151.33],[151.33,151.72],[151.72,151.95],[151.95,152.42],[152.7,152.93],[152.93,153.4],[153.4,153.64],[153.64,153.8],[153.8,154.11],[154.11,154.58]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Kalief Browder was held at Rikers Island for about three years without trial, much of it in solitary confinement, after being accused at 16 of stealing a backpack; the charges were dropped, and he died by suicide in 2015.","start":155.53,"end":171.47,"words":[[155.58,155.97],[155.97,156.44],[156.44,156.68],[156.68,156.99],[156.99,157.15],[157.15,157.62],[157.62,158.09],[158.09,158.25],[158.25,158.64],[158.64,158.88],[158.88,159.19],[159.19,159.66],[159.66,160.21],[160.49,160.8],[160.8,160.96],[160.96,161.12],[161.12,161.27],[161.27,161.9],[161.9,162.85],[163.12,163.44],[163.44,163.83],[163.83,164.3],[164.3,164.46],[164.46,165.01],[165.01,165.16],[165.16,165.64],[165.64,165.79],[165.79,166.34],[166.62,166.77],[166.77,167.32],[167.32,167.48],[167.48,167.95],[168.23,168.46],[168.46,168.62],[168.62,168.94],[168.94,169.17],[169.17,169.72],[169.72,169.88],[169.88,170.9]]},{"el":"h4","text":"What helps","start":172.37,"end":173.41,"words":[[172.42,172.72],[172.72,173.22]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Jury-selection reforms (Washington State's 2018 rule against implicit bias in strikes), fully funded public defense, pretrial release instead of cash bail for low-risk people, and court watching.","start":174.11,"end":187.22,"words":[[174.16,175.2],[175.2,175.65],[175.65,176.25],[176.25,176.69],[176.69,177.58],[177.58,177.81],[177.81,178.25],[178.25,178.85],[178.85,179.22],[179.22,179.37],[179.37,179.96],[180.22,180.52],[180.52,180.97],[180.97,181.41],[181.41,181.93],[182.19,182.86],[182.86,183.24],[183.24,183.68],[183.68,183.83],[183.83,184.05],[184.05,184.35],[184.35,184.5],[184.5,185.02],[185.02,185.47],[185.73,185.95],[185.95,186.17],[186.17,186.69]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Lawyers can take pro bono cases; anyone can join a court-watch program.","start":187.57,"end":193.17,"words":[[187.62,188.01],[188.01,188.24],[188.24,188.55],[188.55,188.85],[188.85,189.32],[189.32,189.86],[190.13,190.59],[190.59,190.82],[190.82,191.21],[191.21,191.36],[191.36,191.9],[191.9,192.59]]}]}